; ======================================================================
; BCM2711 PCIe - the ADDRESSING FOUNDATION and a bench instrument for an
; UNDOCUMENTED register block. Library, not a builtin.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; USE:
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/pcie.pi4"
;
;     If PcieAddrIsWide() = 0
;       ; the compiler truncated the window address - see THE CANARY
;       ; below. Stop. Do not touch the hardware.
;     EndIf
;     v = PcieRcPeek($0000)          ; bounded read of the root complex
;     v = PcieWinPeek($0000)         ; bounded read of the outbound window
;
; DEPENDENCIES - none in code. A library includes no library here
; (project ruling, 2026-07-30), so this file names no other .pi4 and
; computes nothing from one. What it EXPECTS of the caller:
;
;   * a console, if you want to see anything. Every procedure here
;     RETURNS a value; nothing in this file prints.
;   * RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4 IS A LIKELY PREREQUISITE for anything
;     past this file. See THE FIRMWARE DEPENDENCY, below - it is not a
;     maybe, the board's own device tree asserts it.
;
; ======================================================================
;  READ THIS BEFORE YOU READ ANYTHING ELSE IN THIS FILE
; ======================================================================
; THIS FILE DOES NOT BRING UP THE PCIe LINK. It cannot, and the reason
; is not effort or time - it is that THERE IS NO CITABLE SOURCE FOR A
; SINGLE REGISTER OF THE BLOCK AT $FD500000 ANYWHERE ON THIS DISK.
;
; That was checked, not assumed. `bcm2711-peripherals.pdf` is 166 pages
; and mentions PCIe on eight of them. Every one of those eight was read:
;
;   p. 4   contents - lists "6.2.5. ETH_PCIe interrupts" and nothing else
;   p. 5   "BCM2711 contains the following peripherals which may safely
;          be accessed by the ARM" - the list does NOT include PCIe
;   p. 6   Figure 1, the address map. THIS IS THE USEFUL ONE - see below
;   p. 65  "DMA channel 11 is additionally able to access the PCIe
;          interface"
;   p. 86  the interrupt overview block diagram, ETH_PCIe as a source
;   p. 88  IRQ table - "58  OR of all ETH_PCIe L2"
;   p. 89  the five names PCIE_0_INTA/B/C/D and PCIE_0_MSI
;
; The address map, the DMA note and the interrupt lines. NOT ONE
; REGISTER. Not a reset bit, not a link-status bit, not the config-space
; index/data pair, not an inbound-window descriptor. The same document
; says on p. 5 that peripherals "intended to be controlled by the GPU
; ... are omitted from this datasheet. Accessing these peripherals from
; the ARM is not recommended."
;
; So a `PcieBringUp()` in this file would have been ten to twenty
; hand-typed register offsets with no provenance. On this project that
; is not a shortcut, it is the exact failure already on the record for
; today: a store to a real, powered, idle peripheral that produced no
; output and no error, because it was simply the wrong device. A PCIe
; root complex is a worse place to do it than a UART, because there is
; nothing to notice: a write to a wrong offset in an undocumented block
; faults nothing, prints nothing, and leaves you reading a link-status
; bit that is actually somebody else's scratch register.
;
; WHAT THIS FILE IS INSTEAD, and it is deliberately a smaller thing:
;
;   1. THE ADDRESSING. Every number needed to reach the far side of the
;      PCIe bus, each one cited twice where two sources exist, and the
;      conversions between the CPU view and the PCI-bus view - which are
;      NOT the same address and are the classic silent mistake here.
;   2. THE INBOUND ERRATUM, as a predicate a caller can actually call
;      rather than a comment nobody reads.
;   3. THE CANARY. A runtime check that the 64-bit address survived the
;      compiler. Not paranoia - it was failing this morning, silently,
;      and the failure address was $0. See below.
;   4. A BOUNDED, READ-ONLY BENCH INSTRUMENT for the undocumented block,
;      so that the missing citations can be PRODUCED on the bench
;      instead of guessed at here.
;
; It writes nothing to any PCIe register. Not one PokeL in this file.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE CANARY - AND WHY IT SURVIVES ITS OWN GOOD NEWS (2026-08-24)
; ======================================================================
; The whole reason PCIe became approachable is that the Pi 4 backend has
; gone 64-bit: the outbound window is at CPU $6_0000_0000, above 4 GB,
; so with 32-bit pointers the controller was literally unaddressable.
;
; THE WIDENING LANDED WHILE THIS FILE WAS BEING WRITTEN, and both states
; were measured, hours apart, on the same source. Compiling
; `v = PeekL($600000000)`:
;
;   pmfc.exe, 20:18   ->   movz w11, #0
;                          ldr  w12, [x11]
;   pmfc.exe, 20:58   ->   movz x11, #0
;                          movk x11, #6, lsl #32
;                          ldr  w12, [x11]
;
; THE CURRENT pmfc.exe IS CORRECT and this file builds clean against it.
; The first line is not history for its own sake - it is the failure the
; canary exists for, and it is worth knowing exactly how bad it was.
;
; $6_0000_0000 truncated to 32 bits is EXACTLY ZERO, so the read became
; a read of address $0 - the firmware spin table and the secondary
; cores' release mailboxes. There was NO WARNING. The build exited 0 and
; said "pmfc: OK". A poke through the same path would have written the
; spin table, on a board with three cores parked in it.
;
; So the canary is NOT dead code now that the compiler is fixed. It
; guards a property that was false this morning, that no compile-time
; check caught, and that an image already built and sitting on a boot
; stick still has. It costs three comparisons, once.
;
; That is why PcieAddrIsWide() exists and why it should be the FIRST
; thing any caller of this file calls. It is a runtime test, because for
; the bare-expression case a compile-time one does not exist.
;
; The 64-bit backend is CORRECT on both counts that matter here: the
; movz/movk pair above is the right answer, and .i globals come out
; .space 8 rather than .space 4.
;
; --- ONE GOOD SURPRISE, AND IT CHANGES A DESIGN CHOICE HERE ---------
; THIS WHOLE FILE WAS REFUSED BY THE OLD COMPILER, LOUDLY. Built against
; the 20:18 pmfc.exe, the scratch test main did not produce a wrong
; image; it stopped:
;
;   COMPILER ERROR: 'ProcedureReturn 25769803776' in pcieoutcpu() -
;                   the value does not fit the procedure's return width.
;                   ... Every caller would receive 25769803776 silently
;                   WRAPPED to that width - a branch comparing against
;                   it could never be true, with no diagnostic.
;   pmfc: semantic analysis failed
;   pmfc: BUILD FAILED - no output produced.
;
; That is the ProcedureReturn width refusal doing exactly its job, and
; it is a much better outcome than the canary catching it at run time.
;
; NOTE WHY IT FIRED, BECAUSE IT IS THE ARGUMENT FOR A DESIGN CHOICE THAT
; OTHERWISE LOOKS LIKE PADDING. It fired on PcieOutCpu() - a procedure
; that only returns a constant. The bare expression `PeekL($600000000)`
; is NOT caught: that is the case measured above, which compiles clean
; and reads address zero. So wrapping the map in trivial accessors,
; which reads like ceremony, is what converts a silent wrong address
; into a build failure on a compiler that cannot do the job. Keep them,
; and prefer PcieOutCpu() over #PCIE_OUT_CPU in code that a 32-bit build
; might ever see.
;
; The canary stays anyway. The refusal protected against building with a
; 32-bit compiler; the canary protects against everything else.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE ADDRESS MAP - every number below is cited
; ======================================================================
;
; --- THE ROOT COMPLEX'S OWN REGISTERS -------------------------------
; `bcm2711.dtsi` line 553:
;     pcie0: pcie@7d500000 { compatible = "brcm,bcm2711-pcie";
;                            reg = <0x0 0x7d500000 0x9310>; ... }
;
; That $7D500000 is a LEGACY/VC bus address, not an ARM one. The `soc`
; node's ranges translate it - `bcm2711.dtsi` lines 41-43:
;     ranges = <0x7e000000  0x0 0xfe000000  0x01800000>,
;              <0x7c000000  0x0 0xfc000000  0x02000000>,
;              <0x40000000  0x0 0xff800000  0x00800000>;
;
; $7D500000 falls in the second range: $7D500000 - $7C000000 = $1500000,
; and $FC000000 + $1500000 = $FD500000. So the block is at ARM
; $FD500000 and it is $9310 bytes long.
;
; The same arithmetic is what puts the PL011 at $FE201000 (from
; uart0@7e201000 through the FIRST range), and that one is PROVEN on
; silicon - the monitor talks through it. So the translation rule is not
; being taken on trust; it has a working witness on the same chip.
;
; --- THE OUTBOUND WINDOW - CPU $6_0000_0000 -------------------------
; `bcm2711.dtsi` lines 575-576, inside pcie0:
;     ranges = <0x02000000 0x0 0xf8000000  0x6 0x00000000
;               0x0 0x04000000>;
;
; pcie0 has #address-cells = <3> (a PCI address), its parent `scb` has
; #address-cells = <2>, and #size-cells = <2>. So the seven cells split:
;
;     child  (3 cells)   0x02000000 0x0 0xf8000000
;     parent (2 cells)   0x6 0x00000000
;     size   (2 cells)   0x0 0x04000000
;
; CONFIRMED, and this is the reading that was asked for: the window is
; 64 MiB ($0400_0000) at CPU address $6_0000_0000.
;
; ONE THING THAT READING LEAVES OUT, AND IT IS THE DANGEROUS HALF.
; The child cells are not decoration. $02000000 in phys.hi is the PCI
; space code for 32-bit NON-PREFETCHABLE MEMORY, and the two cells after
; it are the address ON THE PCI BUS: $F8000000.
;
;     PCI bus $F800_0000 .. $FBFF_FFFF   <-->   CPU $6_0000_0000 ..
;                                                   $6_03FF_FFFF
;
; A BAR read out of the VL805 will contain a number near $F8000000. That
; number is NOT an address this CPU can load from. Dereferencing a BAR
; value directly lands at $F8000000, which on the ARM side is inside the
; peripheral aperture, not on the PCI bus at all - another store to a
; real, powered, wrong device. PcieCpuFromBus() below is the conversion,
; and it is the reason it exists.
;
; --- CORROBORATION FROM BROADCOM'S OWN DOCUMENT ---------------------
; `bcm2711-peripherals.pdf` p. 6, Figure 1 "BCM2711 Address Maps", the
; "Full 35-bit Address Map" column, lists a region labelled PCIe running
; from 0x6_0000_0000 to 0x7_FFFF_FFFF. p. 5 says in words: "The PCIe
; address range covers 8GB, but the Main peripherals address range only
; covers 64MB."
;
; So $6_0000_0000 is confirmed by two independent sources. Note the
; sizes disagree, and they are not in conflict - they describe different
; things, so all three are recorded here rather than one being picked:
;
;     8 GiB    the SoC's PCIe REGION           peripherals PDF, Fig. 1
;     1 GiB    what the `scb` bus passes       bcm2711.dtsi line 551:
;              through, identity-mapped        <0x6 0x00000000
;                                               0x6 0x00000000
;                                               0x40000000>
;    64 MiB    what pcie0 actually maps        bcm2711.dtsi line 575
;
; 64 MiB IS THE ONE THIS FILE USES, because it is the innermost and the
; smallest, and a window is only as wide as its narrowest translation.
;
; --- THE INBOUND WINDOW, AND THE ERRATUM ----------------------------
; The hardware fact, in our own words; the reference below is a pointer
; to where it can be checked, not a passage carried across. Inside the
; pcie0 node, `bcm2711.dtsi` lines 577-583 record the erratum and size
; the window it forces: the wrapper around the PCIe block cannot reach
; past the first 3 GiB of memory, so that node's inbound translation is
; declared over PCI memory space as bus $0000_0000 to CPU $0000_0000
; with a length of $C000_0000.
;
; Split the same way: PCI bus $0000_0000 maps to CPU $0000_0000, for
; $C000_0000 = 3 GiB. So inbound DMA is IDENTITY-MAPPED and CAPPED at
; 3 GiB. CONFIRMED as read.
;
; THIS IS A HARDWARE ERRATUM AND IT IS THE MOST EXPENSIVE FACT IN THIS
; FILE, because of how it fails. Every XHCI data structure - the device
; context base array, the command ring, every transfer ring, every event
; ring, every data buffer - is memory the CONTROLLER reads, not the CPU.
; Put one at or above $C000_0000 on a 4 GB or 8 GB Pi 4 and the CPU can
; see it perfectly while the controller cannot reach it. The symptom is
; a controller that accepts everything and completes nothing.
;
; This board has 4 GB and its DRAM runs to $FBFFFFFF (measured, U-Boot
; bdinfo - see anvil.pi4's MEMORY MAP). So the top ~940 MiB of
; installed RAM is DMA-POISON for this device, and it is exactly the
; region the monitor's HIGH payload window sits in. A future xhci.pi4
; that allocates its rings "somewhere in the high window" because that
; is where there is room would be wrong in a way no CPU-side test can
; detect. PcieDmaOk() exists to be called before any such allocation is
; handed to the controller.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE FIRMWARE DEPENDENCY - the mailbox is probably not optional
; ======================================================================
; This was flagged as a possibility. The board's own device tree turns
; it into an assertion. `bcm2711-rpi-4-b.dts` lines 209-223:
;
;     &pcie0 {
;         pci@0,0 {
;             device_type = "pci";
;             ...
;             usb@0,0 {
;                 reg = <0 0 0 0 0>;
;                 resets = <&reset RASPBERRYPI_FIRMWARE_RESET_ID_USB>;
;             };
;         };
;     };
;
; Read what that says. The VL805 - `usb@0,0`, the only child of the only
; port - takes its reset FROM THE RASPBERRY PI FIRMWARE, by a firmware
; reset ID, not from any GPIO, register or PCIe mechanism. The provider
; is the VideoCore, and the only channel to the VideoCore is the mailbox.
;
; So on a board revision that needs it, the order is:
;
;     mailbox -> VideoCore loads/resets the VL805 -> the device
;     enumerates -> config space answers
;
; and skipping the first step gives a link that trains to a port with
; nothing behind it. On newer boards the VL805 firmware lives in a SPI
; EEPROM and this step is not needed - so the SAME CODE works on one
; board and not on another, which is the worst version of this problem
; to debug. Do not conclude "no device" from a config read of all-ones
; until the mailbox path has been tried on that specific board.
;
; `RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4` LANDED WHILE THIS WAS BEING WRITTEN, so
; the dependency is no longer hypothetical and the pieces can be named.
; This file does not include it and must not; when the time comes it is
; the MAIN file that lists both. What is already there:
;
;   MailboxBegin(tag, reqBytes, respBytes) / MailboxSetWord /
;   MailboxSend / MailboxWord   - the generic tag transport, so the
;   VL805 reset is a tag number away rather than a new mechanism.
;
;   MailboxBoardRevision()      - and this is the one that matters,
;   because it is how you tell WHICH board you are on, and therefore
;   whether the VideoCore has to load the VL805 firmware at all or
;   whether it comes from the SPI EEPROM. Do not write that decision as
;   a constant; ask the board.
;
; The tag number for the firmware USB reset is NOT recorded here,
; because it is not on this disk. `bcm2711-rpi-4-b.dts` names the
; symbol - RASPBERRYPI_FIRMWARE_RESET_ID_USB - but the header that
; gives it a value is not present, and neither is the tag that carries
; it. That is one more item for the shopping list below.
;
; ======================================================================
;  WHAT IS MISSING, PRECISELY - the shopping list for whoever is next
; ======================================================================
; Not "PCIe is hard". These specific things, none of which are on this
; disk, and all of which are in Linux's drivers/pci/controller/pcie-
; brcmstb.c (which is NOT on this disk either - it was looked for):
;
;   1. The BRIDGE RESET / soft-reset control. Which offset, which bit,
;      which polarity, and how long to hold it.
;   2. The PHY / SERDES enable, and `brcm,enable-ssc` (bcm2711.dtsi line
;      584) - spread-spectrum clocking, which is a register write
;      somewhere in this block. The property is citable; the register
;      behind it is not.
;   3. LINK STATUS. Which offset holds "link up", and which bits give
;      the negotiated width and speed. Without this there is nothing to
;      time out ON - a bounded wait needs a thing to wait for, and this
;      is the missing thing.
;   4. The OUTBOUND WINDOW registers. The dtsi says what the mapping
;      SHOULD be; the hardware has to be told, and those descriptor
;      registers are undocumented.
;   5. The INBOUND WINDOW registers, same problem, plus whatever encodes
;      the 3 GiB cap.
;   6. THE CONFIG-SPACE ACCESS MECHANISM. This is the one that blocks
;      the stated milestone outright. There is no ECAM aperture in the
;      device tree - no `reg-names = "config"`, no second `reg` entry -
;      so config space is reached through an index/data register pair
;      inside the $FD500000 block, and neither offset is known. Config
;      space cannot be read at all until this is.
;
; Items 1-5 are needed for a link. Item 6 is needed to read the ID.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE MILESTONE, AND THE NUMBER TO CHECK IT AGAINST
; ======================================================================
; The first real milestone was correctly identified as reading back the
; VL805's vendor/device ID, because it proves the link, the windows and
; the 64-bit addressing all at once. This file cannot reach it (item 6
; above), but the constants for the CHECK are here so that whoever gets
; config space working has them.
;
; The PCI configuration header layout below is ARCHITECTURAL - PCI Local
; Bus / PCI Express base specification, identical on every PCI device
; ever made, and not Broadcom-specific. It is safe to write down without
; a Broadcom citation because Broadcom does not get a say in it.
;
; THE VL805's ACTUAL ID IS A DIFFERENT MATTER AND IS FLAGGED AS SUCH at
; #PCIE_VL805_ID_EXPECTED below. Read that comment before trusting it.
;
; ======================================================================
;  HOST MODE vs DEVICE MODE - why this file exists at all, given that
; ======================================================================
; The question of whether the XHCI host controller behind PCIe is the
; right FIRST USB target on this board deserves an answer in the file
; rather than only in a report, because the answer is "probably not" and
; somebody will otherwise redo this reasoning.
;
; The Pi 4 has a second, completely separate USB controller: `usb@
; 7e980000` in `bcm283x.dtsi` line 467, compatible "brcm,bcm2835-usb",
; which is a Synopsys DesignWare dwc2 on the USB-C port, and it is
; device-capable. By the same soc-ranges arithmetic as above it is at
; ARM $FE980000, $10000 long.
;
; THE DECIDING DIFFERENCE IS NOT DIFFICULTY, IT IS EVIDENCE. The full
; Linux dwc2 driver is sitting on this disk at Datasheets/pi4/dwc2/ -
; core.c, core.h, gadget.c, hw.h, params.c, platform.c. `hw.h` is a
; complete register map with every offset and every bit field named:
; GOTGCTL $000, GAHBCFG $008, GUSBCFG $00C, GRSTCTL $010, GINTSTS $014,
; GSNPSID $040, GHWCFG1 $044, DCFG $800, DCTL $804, DSTS $808.
;
; And it has the SAME KIND of first milestone, reachable on day one:
; GSNPSID at $040 is the Synopsys core ID, and core.c's
; dwc2_is_controller_alive() is literally "GSNPSID == 0xffffffff means
; no". One PeekL of $FE980040, on-chip, no PCIe bus, no link training,
; no windows, no VideoCore firmware dependency, and no undocumented
; registers - against a milestone here that needs six unciteable things
; to line up first.
;
; So: dwc2 device mode is the better first USB target on this board, on
; evidence rather than on preference. It is a smaller job AND it is the
; one that can be written entirely from citations. That is a
; recommendation, not a decision - nothing has been written for it,
; because it was not what was asked for.
;
; What this file is still worth if that recommendation is taken: the
; addressing, the erratum predicate and the canary are about the PCIe
; BUS, not about XHCI, and they are unchanged by which USB controller
; gets driven first. Nothing here is wasted by that choice.
;
; ======================================================================
;  STATUS - PROVEN ON SILICON 2026-08-25, and load-bearing at every boot
; ======================================================================
; THE VL805 ANSWERED. From the board, `pi4PcieProbe`, 2026-08-25:
;
;     root complex 00:00.0  vendor:device   $271114E4
;     VL805        01:00.0  vendor:device   $34831106
;
; $14E4/$2711 is Broadcom answering as the root complex, which proves
; config space works BEFORE the VL805 read is even attempted; $1106/$3483
; is VIA Labs / VL805 - to the digit, the value #PCIE_VL805_ID_EXPECTED
; has carried since this file was written, as a constant nothing could
; go and check. Two devices, one bus walk.
;
; CORROBORATION WORTH KEEPING: the three addresses this file derived
; from the DEVICE TREE alone - $FD500000, $6_0000_0000, $F8000000 -
; appear in U-Boot's own header as PCIE_REG_BASE, PCIE_CPU_MMIO_WINDOW
; and PCIE_TOP_OF_MEM_WIN, to the digit. Two independent sources
; agreeing, which means the read-only half of this file was right all
; along and the silicon run confirmed rather than discovered it.
;
; AND THIS FILE IS NOW LOAD-BEARING AT EVERY BOOT. The bench boots from
; a USB stick, the stick is behind the VL805, and the VL805 is behind
; this file: PcieInit -> PcieEnumerate -> xhci.pi4 -> usbmsc.pi4 ->
; fat.pi4 is how Anvil reaches KERNEL8.IMG, CONFIG.TXT, its settings
; store and the 609,309-byte CYW43455 firmware image. If anything here
; were wrong the board would not get as far as printing.
;
; MEASURED THE SAME AFTERNOON, once the controller was reachable:
;
;     HCIVERSION $0100   CAPLENGTH 32   maxports 5   maxslots 32
;     HCSPARAMS1 $05000420
;
; Every figure agrees with what U-Boot independently printed for the
; same silicon minutes earlier - "USB XHCI 1.00", "NbrPorts 5",
; "Register 5000420". Not a plausible number; the right one.
;
; TWO FAULTS IN THIS FILE'S OWN CONTRACT WERE FOUND GETTING THERE, and
; each hid the next:
;
;   1. PcieInit asserts PERST, which resets the endpoint and DISCARDS
;      the BAR the FIRMWARE assigned while booting from USB. Nothing
;      re-enumerated, so the caller dereferenced zero and the CPU reset.
;      Fixed by PcieEnumerate - size the BAR, place it, enable memory
;      space and bus master.
;   2. $DEADDEAD came back from the outbound window. Not garbage and not
;      a crash: it is the root complex's own AXI read-error pattern from
;      the register at $4170. The RC is a PCI-TO-PCI BRIDGE and forwards
;      only what falls inside the MEMORY_BASE/LIMIT pair in its own
;      config header - measured at $C000C000, the firmware's old range,
;      while the new BAR sat at $F8000000. THE OUTBOUND WINDOW IS THE
;      CPU SIDE, THE BRIDGE WINDOW IS THE BUS SIDE, AND BOTH ARE
;      REQUIRED. A controller that returns a self-identifying poison
;      value rather than aborting turned a reboot-per-attempt loop into
;      ordinary debugging.
;
;   The page tables took the blame twice and were innocent both times,
;   because they were the most exotic option available and the ordinary
;   ones had not been checked.
;
; AND PcieInit NO LONGER RESETS A LINK THAT IS ALREADY UP. The firmware
; trains this link on a USB-booting board and the loader arrives through
; it, so resetting it reset the controller out from under a live system.
; It ADOPTS a trained link - the same reasoning uart.pi4 gives for
; UartAttach over UartInit.
;
; ---- WHAT IS STILL NOT PROVEN. The first one is structural. ---------
;
;   * THE COLD RESET SEQUENCE HAS NEVER TRAINED A LINK. STATUS read
;     $000000B0 BEFORE PcieInit as well as after, because the firmware
;     had already trained it on a USB-booting board. So the register map
;     and config-space access are proven and THE RESET PATH IS NOT.
;     Settling it needs a boot where the firmware has not already done
;     the work - an SD-card or network boot - which this bench has not
;     run.
;   * THE VL805 FIRMWARE RESET PATH is written and has never executed,
;     for the same reason: the device was already out of reset. The
;     mailbox tag it needs, RPI_FIRMWARE_NOTIFY_XHCI_RESET = $00030058,
;     IS on disk - it arrived in the firmware header downloaded for the
;     framebuffer tags, and mailbox.pi4 recorded it as "not on disk"
;     for a while afterwards because nobody re-checked once the file
;     landed. That note has since been removed. WORTH REMEMBERING AS A
;     SHAPE: a gap can close without the note about the gap changing,
;     which is the same defect class as this status block was.
;   * PcieDmaOk()'s $C0000000 BOUND IS THE PCIe INBOUND WINDOW AND
;     NOTHING ELSE. It is right for this controller and it is NOT a
;     general "safe for DMA" test on this part: the legacy DMA engines
;     are thirty-bit and stop at $40000000, three quarters tighter
;     (bcm2711.dtsi:45, `dma-ranges = <0xc0000000 0x0 0x0 0x40000000>`).
;     A buffer this predicate waves through can still be invisible to
;     the 2D blitter. Measured on silicon 2026-08-26; see the vault note
;     "DMA 2D blitter and the V3D linear question 2026-08-26".
;
; ---- SUPERSEDED, kept rather than deleted ---------------------------
;
;   ~~STATUS - 2026-08-24: NOTHING IN THIS FILE HAS TOUCHED SILICON.~~
;
; True when written and false about thirty hours later, and it stayed in
; the file for three days while the board booted through this code
; several hundred times. The same header also declared the VL805
; milestone unreachable - "NOT ONE SINGLE REGISTER OF THE BLOCK AT
; $FD500000 ANYWHERE ON THIS DISK" - and that half was a CORRECT refusal
; to invent reset sequencing, link training and the config index/data
; pair. It was closed not by difficulty but by fetching U-Boot's
; version-matched sources. Both halves are worth remembering: refusing
; to guess was right, and the status line that shipped beside it went
; stale with nothing in the tree able to notice.
;
; WHAT WAS DONE BEFORE ANY OF IT RAN, so the next reader can weigh it:
;
;   BUILT     from a scratch test main, clean, with the current
;             pmfc.exe:
;               pmfc.exe <test>.pi4 -t pi4 --load-addr 0x200000
;                        -o test.img -s      ->  pmfc: OK
;             20 BSS slots, every one .space 8, none .space 4.
;   REFUSED   by the PREVIOUS pmfc.exe, at semantic analysis, quoted
;             above. Both results are correct and both were wanted.
;   READ      the emitted assembly. $6_0000_0000 materialises as
;             `movz x11, #0` + `movk x11, #6, lsl #32` at all eleven
;             sites; $FD500000 as `movk x11, #64848, lsl #16`;
;             $C0000000 as `movk x11, #49152, lsl #16`, positive in an
;             X register; `(a >> 32)` as `asr x13, x12, x11` with 32 in
;             x11; and the two `mrs` blocks are present.
;
; THE TEST MAIN IS NOT IN THE REPO. It was scratch, it printed the map
; and the self-check over the PL011, and it is not a deliverable -
; anything permanent should live under RaspberryPi4/Examples/ and be
; asked for, not a file that appeared unasked.
;
; THIS FILE REQUIRES THE 64-BIT BACKEND and will not build without it -
; by design, per the refusal above. pmfc.exe carried the 64-bit build
; from 20:58 on 2026-08-24. It was NOT rebuilt by this work; another
; worker landed it, and the before/after measurements above happened to
; straddle that moment.
;
; ~~Specifically NOT verified: that $FD500000 answers a read at all,~~
; ~~that the outbound window answers before the link is trained (it~~
; ~~almost certainly does not - expect all-ones or an abort), and every~~
; ~~number in the address map as seen by the actual bus rather than by~~
; ~~the device tree.~~
;
; ALL THREE OF THOSE WERE SETTLED ON 2026-08-25 and the answers are at
; the top of this block. $FD500000 answers. The address map matches
; U-Boot's own constants. And the middle one turned out to be the wrong
; question on this bench, because the firmware trains the link before
; our first instruction executes - which is exactly why the cold path
; is still the one thing here that has never run.
; ======================================================================

EnableExplicit

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; The root complex register block. bcm2711.dtsi line 555 gives
; reg = <0x0 0x7d500000 0x9310>, translated to the ARM view through the
; soc ranges at lines 41-43. See THE ADDRESS MAP in the header.
;
; THE SIZE IS LOAD-BEARING, not documentation. It is the only bound this
; file has on an undocumented block, and PcieRcPeek() enforces it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PCIE_RC_BASE = $FD500000
#PCIE_RC_SIZE = $9310

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; The outbound window, both ends of it. bcm2711.dtsi line 575.
;
; CPU side and PCI-BUS side are DIFFERENT ADDRESSES. Read the header if
; that sentence looks like pedantry; it is the mistake this file most
; expects the next person to make.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PCIE_OUT_CPU  = $600000000     ; CPU sees the window here - above 4 GB
#PCIE_OUT_SIZE = $04000000      ; 64 MiB
#PCIE_OUT_BUS  = $F8000000      ; the same window's address ON the bus

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Inbound DMA. bcm2711.dtsi lines 577-583 - identity-mapped, capped at
; 3 GiB, and the cap is a documented WRAPPER BUG, not a design choice.
; Anything at or above this that is handed to the controller is
; unreachable BY THE CONTROLLER while looking perfectly fine to the CPU.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PCIE_DMA_LIMIT = $C0000000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PCI configuration header offsets. ARCHITECTURAL - PCI/PCIe base
; specification, every device, every vendor. Not Broadcom's, so not
; subject to the missing-manual problem that stops everything else here.
;
; These are OFFSETS INTO CONFIG SPACE. They are useless until somebody
; finds the index/data pair that reaches config space at all (item 6 in
; the header's shopping list). They are written down now so that the
; hour that mechanism is found, the read is one line away.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PCI_CFG_VENDOR_ID   = $00      ; 16 bits
#PCI_CFG_DEVICE_ID   = $02      ; 16 bits - and $00 as a 32-bit read
                                ; gives (device << 16) | vendor
#PCI_CFG_COMMAND     = $04
#PCI_CFG_STATUS      = $06
#PCI_CFG_REVISION    = $08
#PCI_CFG_CLASS_CODE  = $09      ; 24 bits: prog-if, subclass, class
#PCI_CFG_HEADER_TYPE = $0E
#PCI_CFG_BAR0        = $10
#PCI_CFG_BAR1        = $14
#PCI_CFG_BAR2        = $18
#PCI_CFG_BAR3        = $1C
#PCI_CFG_BAR4        = $20
#PCI_CFG_BAR5        = $24

; COMMAND register bits - architectural, same source.
#PCI_CMD_IO_SPACE     = $0001
#PCI_CMD_MEM_SPACE    = $0002
#PCI_CMD_BUS_MASTER   = $0004

; BAR decoding - architectural. Bit 0 selects the space; for a memory
; BAR, bits 2:1 give the type and 64-bit BARs occupy TWO slots.
#PCI_BAR_SPACE_IO     = $0001   ; bit 0 set = I/O, clear = memory
#PCI_BAR_TYPE_MASK    = $0006   ; bits 2:1
#PCI_BAR_TYPE_64      = $0004   ; bits 2:1 = 10b - a 64-bit memory BAR
#PCI_BAR_MEM_MASK     = $FFFFFFF0
#PCI_BAR_IO_MASK      = $FFFFFFFC

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE VL805's EXPECTED ID - AND ITS PROVENANCE IS WEAK. READ THIS.
;
; The value below is (device << 16) | vendor as a 32-bit read of config
; offset $00: VIA Labs Inc. vendor $1106, device $3483.
;
; THIS IS NOT CITABLE FROM ANYTHING ON THIS DISK. It is not in the
; peripherals PDF, not in the datasheet PDF, and not in any device tree
; here - `bcm2711-rpi-4-b.dts` calls the node `usb@0,0` and gives it
; `reg = <0 0 0 0 0>`, which encodes bus 0 / device 0 / function 0 and
; NO vendor or device ID at all. It is recalled knowledge, written down
; because the milestone needs something to compare against and an
; unverifiable expectation that is LABELLED is more useful than none.
;
; Treat a match as encouraging and a mismatch as INFORMATION, not as a
; failure - print whatever was actually read, always. Nothing in this
; file compares against this constant; that is deliberate.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PCIE_VL805_ID_EXPECTED = $34831106

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Return codes. Distinct negatives, because "it returned -1" sends you
; to look at the bus when the answer was "your offset was odd".
;
; NOT zero and not -1 for everything: a config read can legitimately
; return $FFFFFFFF (that IS the "nobody home" answer on PCI), so a
; refusal has to be distinguishable from a successful read of all-ones.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PCIE_ERR_RANGE   = -1          ; offset outside the block/window
#PCIE_ERR_ALIGN   = -2          ; offset not 4-byte aligned
#PCIE_ERR_NARROW  = -3          ; the compiler truncated the address -
                                ; see PcieAddrIsWide(). THE BAD ONE.

; ======================================================================
;  THE CANARY
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PcieAddrIsWide - did a 64-bit address survive the compiler?
;
; CALL THIS FIRST. Returns 1 if #PCIE_OUT_CPU is intact at run time, 0 if
; it was truncated.
;
; WHY A RUNTIME CHECK FOR A COMPILE-TIME PROPERTY. Because the
; compile-time check is the thing that is missing. Built with a 32-bit
; pmfc, `$600000000` becomes `movz w11, #0` with no diagnostic - the
; build says "pmfc: OK" - and $6_0000_0000 truncated to 32 bits is not a
; wrong-but-harmless address, it is EXACTLY ZERO. A read there hits the
; firmware spin table; a write there hits the release mailboxes of three
; cores that are still spinning in it.
;
; The three tests are not redundant. The first catches total truncation
; (today's actual failure), the second catches a hypothetical 32-bit
; carry into the high word, the third catches anything else at all.
;
; This procedure does not touch hardware and is safe to call anywhere.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieAddrIsWide()
  Protected a.i
  a = #PCIE_OUT_CPU
  If a = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; ">>" is arithmetic on a signed .i. `a` is provably positive here -
  ; it is at most 35 bits - so this is a logical shift in effect.
  If (a >> 32) <> 6
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If a <> #PCIE_OUT_CPU
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE MAP, as procedures
; ======================================================================
; These exist as procedures rather than only as constants so a caller
; can print the map it is actually running with, and so a test can
; compare the two. A constant that is wrong is invisible; a constant
; that is printed is not.

Procedure.i PcieRcBase()
  ProcedureReturn #PCIE_RC_BASE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PcieRcSize()
  ProcedureReturn #PCIE_RC_SIZE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PcieOutCpu()
  ProcedureReturn #PCIE_OUT_CPU
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PcieOutBus()
  ProcedureReturn #PCIE_OUT_BUS
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PcieOutSize()
  ProcedureReturn #PCIE_OUT_SIZE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PcieDmaLimit()
  ProcedureReturn #PCIE_DMA_LIMIT
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PcieCpuFromBus - turn a PCI bus address into one this CPU can load.
;
; THIS IS THE CONVERSION A BAR NEEDS. A BAR programmed into the VL805
; holds a PCI bus address in $F8000000..$FBFFFFFF; the CPU reaches the
; same location at $6_0000_0000 + (bus - $F8000000).
;
; Returns #PCIE_ERR_RANGE if the bus address is outside the window this
; root complex actually maps. It REFUSES rather than returning a
; plausible number, because the plausible number - the bus address used
; directly - lands at $F8000000 on the ARM side, which is inside the
; peripheral aperture. That is a real, powered, completely unrelated
; device, and this project has a case from today of exactly that
; producing no output and no error.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieCpuFromBus(busAddr.i)
  If busAddr < #PCIE_OUT_BUS
    ProcedureReturn #PCIE_ERR_RANGE
  EndIf
  If busAddr >= (#PCIE_OUT_BUS + #PCIE_OUT_SIZE)
    ProcedureReturn #PCIE_ERR_RANGE
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #PCIE_OUT_CPU + (busAddr - #PCIE_OUT_BUS)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PcieBusFromCpu - the inverse. Given a CPU address inside the outbound
; window, what does the device see? Needed when telling a device where
; something is. Refuses anything outside the window.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieBusFromCpu(cpuAddr.i)
  If cpuAddr < #PCIE_OUT_CPU
    ProcedureReturn #PCIE_ERR_RANGE
  EndIf
  If cpuAddr >= (#PCIE_OUT_CPU + #PCIE_OUT_SIZE)
    ProcedureReturn #PCIE_ERR_RANGE
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #PCIE_OUT_BUS + (cpuAddr - #PCIE_OUT_CPU)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PcieDmaOk - may the CONTROLLER read/write this buffer?
;
; THE ERRATUM, AS A CALLABLE PREDICATE. bcm2711.dtsi lines 577-583: the
; PCIe wrapper "has a bug preventing it from accessing beyond the first
; 3GB of memory", and dma-ranges caps inbound at $C0000000.
;
; Returns 1 if the WHOLE buffer is below the cap, 0 otherwise. The whole
; buffer, not its base - a ring that starts at $BFFFF000 and runs 8 KiB
; is half unreachable, and the half that works is the half that makes it
; look like it is working.
;
; Call this before handing ANY address to the controller: the DCBAA, the
; command ring, every transfer ring, every event ring, every data
; buffer. A CPU-side test cannot detect the failure this prevents,
; because from the CPU the memory is completely fine.
;
; len <= 0 is refused rather than waved through. A zero-length DMA
; buffer is a caller bug, and returning 1 for it would mean "yes, that
; is fine" about something that is not.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieDmaOk(addr.i, len.i)
  If len <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If addr < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If addr >= #PCIE_DMA_LIMIT
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (addr + len) > #PCIE_DMA_LIMIT
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE BENCH INSTRUMENT
; ======================================================================
; Read-only, bounded, and deliberately dumb. This is the tool for
; producing the citations the header says are missing - dump the block,
; power-cycle, dump it again, and see what moves. That is how an
; undocumented register map actually gets mapped, and it is a great deal
; safer than writing to offsets on a hunch.
;
; NOTHING HERE WRITES. There is not one PokeL in this file. A write to a
; guessed offset in an undocumented block is the single most expensive
; mistake available on this hardware and it will not be made from here.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PcieRcPeek - read one 32-bit register from the root complex block.
;
; `off` is a byte offset from $FD500000 and is checked against the $9310
; length the device tree gives. Returns the value, or one of the
; #PCIE_ERR_* codes.
;
; PeekL, NOT Peek. Every register in this block is 32 bits wide, and a
; bare Peek is EIGHT bytes on this target now.
;
; THE RETURNED VALUE IS ZERO-EXTENDED, AND THAT WAS CHECKED RATHER THAN
; ASSUMED, because the answer decides whether the #PCIE_ERR_* codes are
; safe. anvil.pi4's header says PeekL sign-extends and that "the
; emitter appends sxtw". That is true, but only on ONE of three paths.
; Compiled with the 64-bit pmfc.exe and read out of the assembly:
;
;   ProcedureReturn PeekL(a)     ldr w12, [x11] / mov x0, x12     ZERO
;   v.i = PeekL(a)               ldr w12, [x11]                   ZERO
;   v.l = PeekL(a)               ldr w12, [x11] / sxtw x12, w12   SIGN
;
; The sxtw comes from the assignment to a .l, not from PeekL. This
; procedure returns the read DIRECTLY, so a register with bit 31 set
; comes back as a positive value in 0..$FFFFFFFF, not as a negative.
;
; THAT IS WHY THE ERROR CODES ARE SMALL NEGATIVES AND IT IS NOT LUCK: no
; 32-bit register value can ever be negative on this path, so a refusal
; is unambiguously distinguishable from data - including from the
; all-ones that PCI returns for "nobody home". Do not "tidy" this into a
; .l local; that would sign-extend the value and put real register
; readings into the same range as the refusals.
;
; THIS DOES NOT KNOW WHETHER THE BLOCK IS POWERED OR CLOCKED. If it is
; not, this either returns rubbish or hangs the bus, and there is no
; citable way from here to find out which. First call on a cold board is
; a real experiment, not a formality - which is the honest reason it is
; a bench instrument and not part of a bring-up sequence.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieRcPeek(off.i)
  If off < 0
    ProcedureReturn #PCIE_ERR_RANGE
  EndIf
  If (off & 3) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn #PCIE_ERR_ALIGN
  EndIf
  If (off + 4) > #PCIE_RC_SIZE
    ProcedureReturn #PCIE_ERR_RANGE
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(#PCIE_RC_BASE + off)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PcieWinPeek - read one 32-bit word through the outbound window.
;
; `off` is a byte offset from CPU $6_0000_0000, bounded by the 64 MiB
; the window maps.
;
; THIS IS THE ONE THAT NEEDS THE CANARY, so it calls it. Built by a
; 32-bit compiler the base address is zero and this would quietly read
; the firmware spin table instead of the PCIe bus; #PCIE_ERR_NARROW is
; returned instead, which is the loud error the project's philosophy
; asks for in place of a silently wrong number.
;
; EXPECT THIS TO RETURN ALL-ONES OR TO ABORT until the link is trained
; and the window is programmed - neither of which this file can do. A
; successful read here is not proof of anything on its own; a read of
; $FFFFFFFF is the normal "no device / no link" answer on PCI and should
; be read that way rather than as a fault.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieWinPeek(off.i)
  If PcieAddrIsWide() = 0
    ProcedureReturn #PCIE_ERR_NARROW
  EndIf
  If off < 0
    ProcedureReturn #PCIE_ERR_RANGE
  EndIf
  If (off & 3) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn #PCIE_ERR_ALIGN
  EndIf
  If (off + 4) > #PCIE_OUT_SIZE
    ProcedureReturn #PCIE_ERR_RANGE
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(#PCIE_OUT_CPU + off)
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  BOUNDED WAITING
; ======================================================================
; Every wait is bounded by a timeout - project rule, and the right one:
; a link that never trains must be REPORTED, not spun on.
;
; THERE IS NOTHING TO WAIT FOR YET. Link training needs a link-status
; bit and that offset is item 3 on the missing list, so the wait itself
; cannot be written. What CAN be written now is the CLOCK it will be
; bounded by, and it is here so that the eventual wait has no excuse to
; be a bare spin count.
;
; RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4 has landed alongside this file and offers
; the same two readings (mailbox.pi4 has its own MbxTicks() too). A library includes no library
; here, so these two tiny readers are duplicated on purpose rather than
; creating a dependency. If timer.pi4 lands with an equivalent, a MAIN
; file may use either; they read the same two registers.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; pcie_Ticks - the ARM architectural counter, all 64 bits of it.
;
; CNTPCT_EL0 is readable at EL2 with no trap and no setup, which makes
; it the cheapest honest clock available to a program a loader started.
; A spin count would have to be calibrated against a CPU frequency the
; firmware is free to change underneath us.
;
; INLINE ASM, AND THIS IS THE "GENUINELY IMPOSSIBLE" CASE the libraries-
; never-intrinsics rule allows for: `mrs` reads a system register and
; the language has no equivalent for it. It is two instructions, it is
; the same pair anvil.pi4 uses, and there is no other way to ask
; the question.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i pcie_Ticks()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntpct_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; pcie_TickHz - the counter's frequency, from CNTFRQ_EL0.
;
; CNTFRQ_EL0 is whatever firmware wrote there, so it can be zero or
; nonsense on a board whose firmware never set it. Callers must sanity-
; check it; PcieTimeoutTicks() below does.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i pcie_TickHz()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntfrq_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PcieTimeoutTicks - how many ticks is `ms` milliseconds?
;
; Falls back to 54000000 if CNTFRQ_EL0 is implausible. That is the
; BCM2711's counter frequency as recorded in anvil.pi4, which uses
; the identical fallback for the identical reason - and note that even
; there it is listed under STILL UNTESTED: "the architectural counter's
; real frequency". So this is a documented assumption, not a measured
; fact, and a timeout built on it may be out by whatever that is out by.
; Being out by a factor is survivable for a timeout; being unbounded is
; not, which is why the fallback exists rather than a refusal.
;
; Refuses ms <= 0 by returning 0, so a caller that passes garbage gets a
; timeout that fires immediately rather than one that never fires.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieTimeoutTicks(ms.i)
  Protected hz.i
  If ms <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  hz = pcie_TickHz()
  If hz <= 0 Or hz > 1000000000
    hz = 54000000
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn (hz / 1000) * ms
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PcieElapsed - ticks since `start`. Wraps correctly for any interval
; short enough to matter: CNTPCT_EL0 is 64 bits and at 54 MHz it takes
; over ten thousand years to wrap, so the subtraction is honest.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieElapsed(start.i)
  ProcedureReturn pcie_Ticks() - start
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  SELF-CHECK
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PcieSelfCheck - does this file's arithmetic agree with itself?
;
; Returns 1 if every internal relationship holds, 0 otherwise. Touches
; no hardware; safe anywhere, including before the canary passes.
;
; WHY THIS IS NOT CEREMONY. The window conversions are the part of this
; file most likely to be edited by somebody adjusting a constant, and an
; off-by-one there produces an address that is wrong by a little - which
; is far worse than one wrong by a lot, because it still lands somewhere
; and still returns a number.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieSelfCheck()
  ; The window round-trips, at both ends and one past the top.
  If PcieCpuFromBus(#PCIE_OUT_BUS) <> #PCIE_OUT_CPU
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If PcieBusFromCpu(#PCIE_OUT_CPU) <> #PCIE_OUT_BUS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If PcieCpuFromBus(#PCIE_OUT_BUS + #PCIE_OUT_SIZE - 4) <> (#PCIE_OUT_CPU + #PCIE_OUT_SIZE - 4)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If PcieCpuFromBus(#PCIE_OUT_BUS + #PCIE_OUT_SIZE) <> #PCIE_ERR_RANGE
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If PcieCpuFromBus(#PCIE_OUT_BUS - 4) <> #PCIE_ERR_RANGE
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; The erratum predicate, on both sides of the cap.
  If PcieDmaOk(#PCIE_DMA_LIMIT - 4096, 4096) <> 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If PcieDmaOk(#PCIE_DMA_LIMIT - 2048, 4096) <> 0    ; straddles the cap
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If PcieDmaOk(#PCIE_DMA_LIMIT, 4) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If PcieDmaOk($40000000, 0) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; The bounds on the two readers. Offsets, not hardware - these do not
  ; reach the bus, they are refused before they get there.
  If PcieRcPeek(-4) <> #PCIE_ERR_RANGE
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If PcieRcPeek(2) <> #PCIE_ERR_ALIGN
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If PcieRcPeek(#PCIE_RC_SIZE) <> #PCIE_ERR_RANGE
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  BRING-UP - added 2026-08-25, and the reason it could not exist before
; ======================================================================
;  Everything above this line is READ-ONLY on purpose. The header says,
;  in as many words, that there is NOT ONE SINGLE REGISTER OF THE BLOCK
;  AT $FD500000 ANYWHERE ON THIS DISK, and that reset sequencing, link
;  training, the window descriptors and the config-space index/data pair
;  would all have to be invented. So they were not written.
;
;  THAT SENTENCE IS NOW FALSE, and this is the correction. U-Boot does
;  all of this and its sources say how. They are on disk now, and
;  VERSION-MATCHED to the build actually running on this board - the
;  console reports U-Boot 2025.01-3+deb13u1, and master's PCIe driver
;  DIFFERS from 2025.01, which was checked rather than assumed:
;
;    Datasheets/pi4/uboot/bcm2711_acpi.h            61 register defines
;    Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_pcie_brcmstb.c   brcm_pcie_probe()
;
;  EVERY REGISTER BELOW IS CITED to that header by name. None is
;  inferred and none is guessed.
;
;  CORROBORATION, worth recording. The three addresses this file had
;  already derived from the DEVICE TREE alone - the block at $FD500000,
;  the CPU window at $6_0000_0000, the bus window at $F8000000 - appear
;  in that header as PCIE_REG_BASE, PCIE_CPU_MMIO_WINDOW and
;  PCIE_TOP_OF_MEM_WIN, to the digit. Two independent sources agreeing
;  is a far better position than either alone, and it means the
;  read-only half above was right all along.
;
;  DEPENDS ON timer.pi4 for delay(). Include it before this file.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---- registers, every one from bcm2711_acpi.h ------------------------
#PCIE_RGR1_SW_INIT_1              = $9210
#PCIE_SW_INIT_1_INIT_MASK         = 2
#PCIE_SW_INIT_1_PERST_MASK        = 1
#PCIE_MISC_MISC_CTRL              = $4008
#PCIE_MISC_CTRL_SCB_ACCESS_EN     = $1000
#PCIE_MISC_CTRL_CFG_READ_UR_MODE  = $2000
#PCIE_MISC_CTRL_MAX_BURST_MASK    = $300000
#PCIE_MISC_CTRL_SCB0_SIZE_MASK    = $F8000000
#PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR1_CONFIG_LO      = $402C
#PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR2_CONFIG_LO      = $4034
#PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR2_CONFIG_HI      = $4038
#PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR3_CONFIG_LO      = $403C
#PCIE_RC_BAR_SIZE_MASK            = $1F
#PCIE_MISC_PCIE_STATUS            = $4068
#PCIE_STATUS_PHYLINKUP_MASK       = $10
#PCIE_STATUS_DL_ACTIVE_MASK       = $20
#PCIE_MISC_HARD_PCIE_HARD_DEBUG   = $4204
#PCIE_HARD_DEBUG_SERDES_IDDQ      = $08000000
#PCIE_MSI_INTR2_CLR               = $4508
#PCIE_MSI_INTR2_MASK_SET          = $4510
#PCIE_MISC_WIN0_LO                = $400C
#PCIE_MISC_WIN0_HI                = $4010
#PCIE_MISC_WIN0_BASE_LIMIT        = $4070
#PCIE_MISC_WIN0_BASE_HI           = $4080
#PCIE_MISC_WIN0_LIMIT_HI          = $4084
#PCIE_RC_CFG_PRIV1_ID_VAL3        = $043C
#PCIE_ID_VAL3_CLASS_CODE_MASK     = $FFFFFF
#PCIE_CLASS_BRIDGE_PCI            = $060400
#PCIE_RC_CFG_VENDOR_REG1          = $0188
#PCIE_VENDOR_REG1_ENDIAN_MASK     = $C

; THE CONFIG-SPACE INDEX/DATA PAIR. These two numbers are the whole
; reason this file was blocked: there is no ECAM aperture in the device
; tree, so config space is unreachable without them, and they appear in
; no Broadcom document. brcm_pcie_config_address() writes the index,
; then accesses DATA + (offset & $FFF).
#PCIE_EXT_CFG_INDEX               = $9000
#PCIE_EXT_CFG_DATA                = $8000

#PCIE_ERR_LINK      = -4        ; the link never came up
#PCIE_ERR_EP_MODE   = -5        ; controller is in endpoint, not root
#PCIE_ERR_CFG_BDF   = -6        ; bus/device/function or offset bad
; Declared HERE and not with the enumeration codes below, because a
; constant has to exist before the line that uses it and PcieInit is
; above them. Putting it there parsed as 'Expected EndProcedure' on
; the ProcedureReturn underneath the unknown name, which points at a
; line that is perfectly fine.
#PCIE_ERR_DMA_OFFSET = -10      ; the inbound DMA offset would not
                                ; zero, so the endpoint cannot reach
                                ; system memory at all

; 1 GiB inbound, encoded as U-Boot does it: ilog2(size) - 15, so
; 30 - 15 = 15. A bare literal because a constant initialiser here
; takes no arithmetic.
#PCIE_BAR2_SIZE_1G  = 15

Global pcie_up.i
Global pcie_err.i

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  pcie_Poke / pcie_Modify - the write side, bounded like the read side.
;
;  The read accessors above refuse an out-of-range or unaligned offset
;  rather than trusting the caller, and a WRITE has strictly more reason
;  to: a stray poke into a controller block does not hand back a wrong
;  number you might notice, it changes how the machine behaves some time
;  later.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure pcie_Poke(off.i, v.i)
  If off < 0 Or (off & 3) <> 0 Or (off + 4) > #PCIE_RC_SIZE
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  PokeN(#PCIE_RC_BASE + off, v)
EndProcedure

Procedure pcie_Modify(off.i, clearMask.i, setMask.i)
  Define v.i
  v = PcieRcPeek(off)
  If v < 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  v = (v & (~clearMask)) | setMask
  pcie_Poke(off, v)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PcieLinkUp - is the link trained?
;
;  BOTH BITS, not either. brcm_pcie_link_up() requires PHYLINKUP and
;  DL_ACTIVE together: the physical layer can be up while the data link
;  is not, and a driver that accepts the first alone goes on to talk to
;  a link that cannot carry a transaction.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieLinkUp()
  Define v.i
  v = PcieRcPeek(#PCIE_MISC_PCIE_STATUS)
  If v < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (v & #PCIE_STATUS_PHYLINKUP_MASK) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (v & #PCIE_STATUS_DL_ACTIVE_MASK) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PcieCfgRead32 / PcieCfgWrite32 - PCI configuration space.
;
;  bus 0 device 0 is the root complex itself and is reached directly;
;  anything else goes through the index/data pair. That split is
;  brcm_pcie_config_address(), not an invention.
;
;  A READ OF $FFFFFFFF IS THE NORMAL "nothing there" ANSWER on PCI and
;  must not be treated as a fault. An absent device is an absence, not
;  an error, and every bus scan depends on telling the two apart. Step 3
;  of PcieInit sets CFG_READ_UR_MODE precisely so an empty slot answers
;  all-ones instead of aborting.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i pcie_CfgIndex(bus.i, dev.i, fn.i)
  ProcedureReturn (bus << 20) | (dev << 15) | (fn << 12)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PcieCfgRead32(bus.i, dev.i, fn.i, off.i)
  If bus < 0 Or dev < 0 Or fn < 0 Or off < 0 Or (off & 3) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn #PCIE_ERR_CFG_BDF
  EndIf
  If bus = 0 And dev = 0
    ProcedureReturn PcieRcPeek(off & $FFF)
  EndIf
  pcie_Poke(#PCIE_EXT_CFG_INDEX, pcie_CfgIndex(bus, dev, fn))
  ProcedureReturn PcieRcPeek(#PCIE_EXT_CFG_DATA + (off & $FFF))
EndProcedure

Procedure PcieCfgWrite32(bus.i, dev.i, fn.i, off.i, v.i)
  If bus < 0 Or dev < 0 Or fn < 0 Or off < 0 Or (off & 3) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If bus = 0 And dev = 0
    pcie_Poke(off & $FFF, v)
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  pcie_Poke(#PCIE_EXT_CFG_INDEX, pcie_CfgIndex(bus, dev, fn))
  pcie_Poke(#PCIE_EXT_CFG_DATA + (off & $FFF), v)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PcieInit - reset the controller, train the link, map the window.
;
;  The order is brcm_pcie_probe()'s and it is not negotiable. Returns 1,
;  or 0 with PcieError()/PcieErrorText() set. Safe to call twice.
;
;  TIMING IS ON THE ARCHITECTURAL COUNTER, not a spin loop. This
;  sequence has real 100 microsecond and 100 millisecond waits in it,
;  and delay() in timer.pi4 was MEASURED clock-independent to 0 parts
;  per thousand across a 2.5x CPU clock change. A calibrated spin would
;  be wrong by that factor the moment somebody calls ClockInit() - which
;  the monitor now does.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global pcie_adopted.i

; ======================================================================
;  WHICH PART OF THE BRING-UP IS RUNNING - added 2026-09-11.
;
;  THE SAME REASONING xhci.pi4 CARRIES, and here it buys more, because
;  this file has TWO paths and a board only ever takes one of them. A
;  monitor entered from a running monitor finds the link already trained
;  and ADOPTS it: three register writes and out. A monitor the firmware
;  entered from power-on may take the COLD path - PERST, the SerDes out
;  of IDDQ, the control registers, the inbound BAR, MSI masked, PERST
;  released and up to 100 ms of link polling. Everything in that second
;  list is reached on a cold flash boot and on nothing else, so a fault
;  in it cannot be reproduced by entering a second monitor into RAM, and
;  the only instrument that can tell the two paths apart from a panel is
;  a marker that says which one was taken.
;
;  The hook takes no argument and the phase is read back through
;  PciePhase(), which is genet.pi4's shape and the one this tree has run
;  on silicon. The marker is file-scope storage because automatic storage
;  lives in the invocation's frame as of 2026-09-11 and a marker whose
;  job is to outlive the call that set it cannot live there.
; ======================================================================
#PCIE_PH_IDLE     = 0
#PCIE_PH_CANARY   = 1    ; the 64-bit window-base canary
#PCIE_PH_PROBE    = 2    ; asking whether the link is already up
#PCIE_PH_ADOPT    = 3    ; adopt: the outbound window
#PCIE_PH_INBOUND  = 4    ; adopt: the RC_BAR2 offset, and its readback
#PCIE_PH_PERST    = 5    ; cold: INIT and PERST asserted
#PCIE_PH_SERDES   = 6    ; cold: the SerDes out of IDDQ
#PCIE_PH_CTRL     = 7    ; cold: burst size, SCB, inbound BAR, MSI masked
#PCIE_PH_TRAIN    = 8    ; cold: PERST released, waiting for the link
#PCIE_PH_WINDOW   = 9    ; cold: the outbound window
#PCIE_PH_UP       = 10   ; the link is up either way
#PCIE_PH_BUSNUM   = 11   ; the bridge's secondary/subordinate numbers
#PCIE_PH_ENUM     = 12   ; the endpoint's id, BAR and command register
#PCIE_PH_DONE     = 13

Global pcie_phaseAt.i = #PCIE_PH_IDLE
Global *pcie_phaseHook = 0

Procedure.i PciePhase() : ProcedureReturn pcie_phaseAt : EndProcedure

Procedure.i PciePhaseText(p.i)
  Select p
    Case #PCIE_PH_CANARY  : ProcedureReturn "canary"
    Case #PCIE_PH_PROBE   : ProcedureReturn "probe"
    Case #PCIE_PH_ADOPT   : ProcedureReturn "adopt"
    Case #PCIE_PH_INBOUND : ProcedureReturn "inbound"
    Case #PCIE_PH_PERST   : ProcedureReturn "perst"
    Case #PCIE_PH_SERDES  : ProcedureReturn "serdes"
    Case #PCIE_PH_CTRL    : ProcedureReturn "control"
    Case #PCIE_PH_TRAIN   : ProcedureReturn "train"
    Case #PCIE_PH_WINDOW  : ProcedureReturn "window"
    Case #PCIE_PH_UP      : ProcedureReturn "up"
    Case #PCIE_PH_BUSNUM  : ProcedureReturn "busnum"
    Case #PCIE_PH_ENUM    : ProcedureReturn "endpoint"
    Case #PCIE_PH_DONE    : ProcedureReturn "ok"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "idle"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PcieSetPhaseHook(*fn)
  Define old.i
  old = *pcie_phaseHook
  *pcie_phaseHook = *fn
  ProcedureReturn old
EndProcedure

Procedure pcie_Phase(p.i)
  pcie_phaseAt = p
  If *pcie_phaseHook <> 0
    pcie_phaseHook()
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PcieProgramWindow - point the outbound window at our bus range.
;
;  Factored out because BOTH paths through PcieInit need it: a cold
;  link after training, and an already-trained link we are adopting.
;  The firmware sets this up for its own use and nothing says its
;  choice matches ours.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure PcieProgramWindow()
  Define v.i
  Define lim.i
  ; the outbound window: CPU $6_0000_0000 -> bus $F8000000, 64 MiB.
  ;    LO/HI take the BUS address. BASE_LIMIT and the two HI registers
  ;    take the CPU address in 1 MiB units, which is why everything is
  ;    shifted right by 20.
  lim = #PCIE_OUT_CPU + #PCIE_OUT_SIZE - 1
  pcie_Poke(#PCIE_MISC_WIN0_LO, #PCIE_OUT_BUS)
  pcie_Poke(#PCIE_MISC_WIN0_HI, 0)
  v = ((#PCIE_OUT_CPU >> 20) & $FFF) << 4
  v = v | (((lim >> 20) & $FFF) << 20)
  pcie_Poke(#PCIE_MISC_WIN0_BASE_LIMIT, v)
  pcie_Poke(#PCIE_MISC_WIN0_BASE_HI, (#PCIE_OUT_CPU >> 32) & $FF)
  pcie_Poke(#PCIE_MISC_WIN0_LIMIT_HI, (lim >> 32) & $FF)

EndProcedure

Procedure.i PcieInit()
  Define i.i
  Define ibLo.i                 ; RC_BAR2 readback, adopt path
  Define ibHi.i
  Define v.i
  Define lim.i

  pcie_err = 0
  If pcie_up <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; The canary first. Built by a 32-bit compiler the window base
  ; truncates to zero and every access below would land on the firmware
  ; spin table instead of the bus.
  pcie_Phase(#PCIE_PH_CANARY)
  If PcieAddrIsWide() = 0
    pcie_err = #PCIE_ERR_NARROW
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; --- DO NOT RESET A LINK THAT IS ALREADY UP -------------------------
  ; MEASURED, and it cost two boots. This board BOOTS FROM USB: the
  ; firmware trains this link and enumerates the VL805 before anything of
  ; ours runs, and U-Boot is itself loaded through it. Asserting PERST
  ; resets that controller out from under a live system.
  ;
  ; The symptom was not immediate. The probe ran, printed, returned to
  ; the monitor cleanly - and then the board reset on its own a second or
  ; two later, twice, in the gap before the next command. A fault that
  ; arrives after the program has finished is the worst kind to attribute,
  ; and it was only obvious once the same gap failed twice running.
  ;
  ; So the reset is now CONDITIONAL. If the link is already trained,
  ; adopt it: skip the reset, skip PERST, and go straight to programming
  ; the window. That is the same reasoning uart.pi4 uses for UartAttach
  ; rather than UartInit - a program started by something that has
  ; already configured the hardware should adopt it, not re-initialise
  ; it and throw away what the previous owner set up.
  ;
  ; The full sequence below is still correct and still needed for a cold
  ; link. It is simply not what this board needs, and finding that out on
  ; a board that boots some other way is a different day's work.
  pcie_Phase(#PCIE_PH_PROBE)
  If PcieLinkUp() <> 0
    pcie_adopted = 1
    pcie_Phase(#PCIE_PH_ADOPT)
    ; The window still has to be right - it is ours to use and nothing
    ; guarantees the firmware left it where we want it.
    PcieProgramWindow()

    ; ---- AND THE INBOUND OFFSET, WHICH IS NOT OURS EITHER -----------
    ; This block exists because leaving it out cost a night of blaming
    ; a USB controller. XhciInit reached Run/Stop and came back with
    ; USBSTS $15 - Host System Error, the controller reporting that its
    ; OWN memory access failed - while reads of that same controller
    ; had been working perfectly all evening.
    ;
    ; RC_BAR2 does not hold a base address. It holds an OFFSET that is
    ; ADDED to a system address to make the PCI bus address a device
    ; must use. v2025.01_pcie_brcmstb.c, brcm_pcie_probe():
    ;
    ;     rc_bar2_offset = region.bus_start - region.phys_start;
    ;
    ; U-Boot had left $4_0000_0000 in it. Every DMA address in this
    ; project is a plain system address, so every one of them was short
    ; by four gigabytes and landed outside the window. The device could
    ; not reach us at all, which is precisely what HSE means.
    ;
    ; The non-adopt path below programs offset zero and never had the
    ; problem. This board never takes that path - the firmware brings
    ; the link up to boot from USB - so the identity mapping the rest of
    ; the code assumes was true in the branch we never run and false in
    ; the branch we always run.
    ;
    ; ZERO THE OFFSET, KEEP THE SIZE. The size code is the low five bits
    ; and belongs to whoever sized the window; re-deriving it would mean
    ; possibly shrinking the reachable range for no reason. Only the
    ; offset is wrong, so only the offset is touched.
    ;
    ; THE ONLY DEVICE BEHIND THIS ROOT COMPLEX IS THE VL805, soldered
    ; to the board, and it is the device being taken over. So there is
    ; no third party whose mapping is being changed underneath it.
    pcie_Phase(#PCIE_PH_INBOUND)
    ibLo = PcieRcPeek(#PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR2_CONFIG_LO) & $FFFFFFFF
    pcie_Poke(#PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR2_CONFIG_LO, ibLo & $1F)
    pcie_Poke(#PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR2_CONFIG_HI, 0)

    ; READ IT BACK. A write to this block that does not take is exactly
    ; the failure being fixed, and it is invisible from the CPU side.
    ibLo = PcieRcPeek(#PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR2_CONFIG_LO) & $FFFFFFFF
    ibHi = PcieRcPeek(#PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR2_CONFIG_HI) & $FFFFFFFF
    If (ibLo & $FFFFFFE0) <> 0 Or ibHi <> 0
      pcie_err = #PCIE_ERR_DMA_OFFSET
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    ; A size code of zero means the window is off, and nothing this
    ; library does afterwards can work. Better here than as an HSE.
    If (ibLo & $1F) = 0
      pcie_err = #PCIE_ERR_DMA_OFFSET
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf

    pcie_up = 1
    pcie_Phase(#PCIE_PH_UP)
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; 1. assert INIT and PERST together, hold, release INIT only.
  pcie_Phase(#PCIE_PH_PERST)
  pcie_Modify(#PCIE_RGR1_SW_INIT_1, 0, #PCIE_SW_INIT_1_INIT_MASK | #PCIE_SW_INIT_1_PERST_MASK)
  delay(1)
  pcie_Modify(#PCIE_RGR1_SW_INIT_1, #PCIE_SW_INIT_1_INIT_MASK, 0)

  ; 2. take the SerDes out of IDDQ - the PHY is powered down out of reset
  pcie_Phase(#PCIE_PH_SERDES)
  pcie_Modify(#PCIE_MISC_HARD_PCIE_HARD_DEBUG, #PCIE_HARD_DEBUG_SERDES_IDDQ, 0)
  delay(1)

  ; 3. burst size 128, SCB access on, and CFG_READ_UR_MODE. The last
  ;    makes an unsupported-request config read return all-ones instead
  ;    of aborting, which is what lets a bus scan probe an empty slot
  ;    without taking a fault. PcieCfgRead32 depends on it.
  pcie_Phase(#PCIE_PH_CTRL)
  pcie_Modify(#PCIE_MISC_MISC_CTRL, #PCIE_MISC_CTRL_MAX_BURST_MASK, #PCIE_MISC_CTRL_SCB_ACCESS_EN | #PCIE_MISC_CTRL_CFG_READ_UR_MODE)

  ; 4. inbound BAR2 - where the controller may reach into OUR memory.
  ;    dma-ranges in bcm2711.dtsi is identity-mapped and capped at
  ;    $C0000000, which is the erratum PcieDmaOk exists for, so the
  ;    offset is zero and only the size field matters.
  pcie_Poke(#PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR2_CONFIG_LO, #PCIE_BAR2_SIZE_1G)
  pcie_Poke(#PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR2_CONFIG_HI, 0)
  pcie_Modify(#PCIE_MISC_MISC_CTRL, #PCIE_MISC_CTRL_SCB0_SIZE_MASK, #PCIE_BAR2_SIZE_1G << 27)
  ;    BAR1 and BAR3 disabled - size field zero
  pcie_Modify(#PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR1_CONFIG_LO, #PCIE_RC_BAR_SIZE_MASK, 0)
  pcie_Modify(#PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR3_CONFIG_LO, #PCIE_RC_BAR_SIZE_MASK, 0)

  ; 5. mask and clear MSI. We use no interrupts, and an unmasked
  ;    controller with no handler is a hang waiting to happen.
  pcie_Poke(#PCIE_MSI_INTR2_MASK_SET, $FFFFFFFF)
  pcie_Poke(#PCIE_MSI_INTR2_CLR, $FFFFFFFF)

  ; 6. release PERST - the device may now leave reset - and wait.
  ;    U-Boot allows 100 ms in 5 ms steps after a flat 100 ms settle.
  pcie_Phase(#PCIE_PH_TRAIN)
  pcie_Modify(#PCIE_RGR1_SW_INIT_1, #PCIE_SW_INIT_1_PERST_MASK, 0)
  delay(100)
  i = 0
  While i < 100
    If PcieLinkUp() <> 0
      Break
    EndIf
    delay(5)
    i = i + 5
  Wend

  If PcieLinkUp() = 0
    pcie_err = #PCIE_ERR_LINK
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  pcie_Phase(#PCIE_PH_WINDOW)
  PcieProgramWindow()

  ; 8. class code to PCI-to-PCI bridge, and little-endian BAR2.
  pcie_Modify(#PCIE_RC_CFG_PRIV1_ID_VAL3, #PCIE_ID_VAL3_CLASS_CODE_MASK, #PCIE_CLASS_BRIDGE_PCI)
  pcie_Modify(#PCIE_RC_CFG_VENDOR_REG1, #PCIE_VENDOR_REG1_ENDIAN_MASK, 0)

  pcie_up = 1
  pcie_Phase(#PCIE_PH_UP)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; 1 if PcieInit ADOPTED an already-trained link rather than resetting it.
Procedure.i PcieAdopted()
  ProcedureReturn pcie_adopted
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PcieError()
  ProcedureReturn pcie_err
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PcieErrorText()
  Select pcie_err
    Case 0
      ProcedureReturn "pcie: ok"
    Case #PCIE_ERR_NARROW
      ProcedureReturn "pcie: window base truncated to 32 bits - built by a 32-bit compiler"
    Case #PCIE_ERR_DMA_OFFSET
      ProcedureReturn "pcie: the inbound DMA offset would not zero - the endpoint cannot reach system memory, and any DMA it attempts will report a host system error"
    Case #PCIE_ERR_LINK
      ProcedureReturn "pcie: link never came up - PHYLINKUP and DL_ACTIVE are both required"
    Case #PCIE_ERR_EP_MODE
      ProcedureReturn "pcie: controller is in endpoint mode, not root complex"
    Case #PCIE_ERR_CFG_BDF
      ProcedureReturn "pcie: bad bus/device/function, or unaligned config offset"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "pcie: unknown error"
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PcieVl805Id - THE MILESTONE.
;
;  vendor:device from bus 1 device 0 function 0. Expect $34831106 - VIA
;  Labs $1106, VL805 $3483 - which #PCIE_VL805_ID_EXPECTED has carried
;  since this file was written read-only, as a number nothing could yet
;  go and check.
;
;  $FFFFFFFF means the link IS up and nothing answered at that address.
;  That is a different failure from a link that never trained, and is
;  worth reporting as itself.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieVl805Id()
  ProcedureReturn PcieCfgRead32(1, 0, 0, 0)
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  ENUMERATION - assign the endpoint a BAR and switch it on
; ======================================================================
;  ADDED 2026-08-25, AFTER THIS LIBRARY CRASHED THE BOARD.
;
;  pi4XhciSelfTest took a data abort and reset the CPU. The predicted
;  cause was the loader's page tables not mapping the outbound window.
;  It was not that. A diagnostic that read config space and deliberately
;  did NOT dereference anything found:
;
;      command/stat   $00100000     memory space DISABLED
;      BAR0 raw       $00000004     type bits only - NO ADDRESS
;
;  THE ENDPOINT HAD NO BAR, AND THIS FILE IS WHY. PcieInit asserts PERST,
;  which resets the device and discards the configuration the FIRMWARE
;  had given it while booting from USB. U-Boot saw three USB devices
;  because U-Boot had enumerated the bus; we reset the device and then
;  never did. XHCI dereferenced a zero base and the CPU aborted.
;
;  Bringing a link up is therefore only half the job. A PCI device out of
;  reset answers config space and NOTHING ELSE: its registers are
;  unreachable until software decides where they live, writes that
;  address into a BAR, and sets the memory-space enable. That is
;  enumeration, and it is what U-Boot's auto-configuration pass does.
;
;  DELIBERATELY MINIMAL. This assigns ONE 64-bit memory BAR to ONE
;  endpoint at bus 1, which is the whole population of this bus - the
;  root complex has exactly one child and it is soldered to the board.
;  No recursive scan, no bridges behind bridges, no I/O space, no
;  expansion ROM. Those are real parts of PCI and none of them exists
;  here; writing them would be inventing a general enumerator to run
;  against a fixed single device.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; THE PCI HEADER CONSTANTS ARE ALREADY AT THE TOP OF THIS FILE -
; #PCI_CFG_COMMAND, #PCI_CFG_BAR0/1, #PCI_CMD_MEM_SPACE,
; #PCI_CMD_BUS_MASTER, #PCI_BAR_TYPE_MASK, #PCI_BAR_TYPE_64. The first
; draft of this section redefined all six, and the compiler refused the
; build naming the one whose VALUE differed: #PCI_BAR_TYPE_MASK as 6
; against 15. Worth recording, because five of the six were identical
; and would have merged silently under a compiler that allowed it - and
; the sixth is the mask that decides whether a BAR is read as 64-bit.

#PCIE_ERR_NO_ENDPOINT = -7      ; nothing answered at bus 1
#PCIE_ERR_BAR_SIZE    = -8      ; the BAR reported an impossible size
#PCIE_ERR_BAR_SPACE   = -9      ; it will not fit the outbound window

Global pcie_barBus.i            ; where the BAR was placed, ON THE BUS
Global pcie_barCpu.i            ; the same place, as this CPU sees it
Global pcie_barSize.i

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PcieBarSize - how much space does this BAR want?
;
;  The standard probe, and it is destructive by design: write all-ones,
;  read back, and the device returns its own address-decoder mask - the
;  low bits it does NOT decode read back as zero. Size is that mask
;  inverted, plus one.
;
;  THE ORIGINAL VALUE MUST BE PUT BACK by the caller. On a device that
;  has already been assigned an address, leaving all-ones in the BAR
;  aims its decoder at the top of memory. Here the BAR is zero anyway,
;  but a routine that only works because its input happened to be
;  useless is a trap for whoever calls it next.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieBarSize(bus.i, dev.i, fn.i, off.i)
  Define orig.i
  Define mask.i
  orig = PcieCfgRead32(bus, dev, fn, off)
  PcieCfgWrite32(bus, dev, fn, off, $FFFFFFFF)
  mask = PcieCfgRead32(bus, dev, fn, off)
  PcieCfgWrite32(bus, dev, fn, off, orig)
  If mask = 0 Or mask = -1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  mask = mask & $FFFFFFF0
  ; ~mask + 1 over the low 32 bits. Masked back to 32 because .i is 64
  ; bits here and the inversion would otherwise set every high bit.
  ProcedureReturn ((~mask) + 1) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PcieEnumerate - give the endpoint an address and turn it on.
;
;  Returns 1, or 0 with PcieError() set. Call after PcieInit. Idempotent:
;  a second call re-reads what it wrote and returns the same answer.
;
;  THE ADDRESS IS A BUS ADDRESS, NOT A CPU ADDRESS, and getting that
;  backwards is the classic error this library was built around. The BAR
;  is decoded by the DEVICE, which lives on the PCI bus and has never
;  heard of $6_0000_0000. It goes at the bottom of the outbound window's
;  BUS view - $F8000000 - and the window hardware translates. The CPU
;  reaches the same registers at PcieCpuFromBus() of that.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieEnumerate()
  Define id.i
  Define sz.i
  Define cmd.i
  Define lo.i
  Define is64.i

  pcie_err = 0
  If pcie_up = 0
    pcie_err = #PCIE_ERR_LINK
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  pcie_Phase(#PCIE_PH_BUSNUM)

  ; ---- THE BRIDGE'S BUS NUMBERS, BEFORE ANYTHING ON BUS 1 IS READ ----
  ; A PCI-to-PCI bridge only forwards a configuration cycle downstream
  ; if the target bus is inside its SECONDARY..SUBORDINATE range. Out of
  ; reset that range is 0..0, which contains no bus at all, so every
  ; read of bus 1 goes nowhere and comes back as all-ones.
  ;
  ; ADDED 2026-08-26, WHEN ANVIL BECAME THE BOOT IMAGE. This never
  ; showed up before because the board always came up under U-Boot,
  ; which had already written $00010100 here - primary 0, secondary 1,
  ; subordinate 1 - and PcieInit's adopt path inherited it. Booted
  ; straight from the firmware, PcieInit takes its own cold path, the
  ; link trains correctly, and then the very first config read of the
  ; endpoint returns $FFFFFFFF because nothing was ever told that bus 1
  ; exists.
  ;
  ; Offset $18 on a bridge header: primary in bits 7:0, secondary in
  ; 15:8, subordinate in 23:16. One device, one bus behind us, so
  ; secondary and subordinate are both 1.
  Define busno.i
  busno = PcieCfgRead32(0, 0, 0, $18)
  If ((busno >> 8) & $FF) = 0
    PcieCfgWrite32(0, 0, 0, $18, (busno & $FF000000) | $00010100)
  EndIf

  id = PcieCfgRead32(1, 0, 0, 0)
  If id = -1 Or id = 0
    pcie_err = #PCIE_ERR_NO_ENDPOINT
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  pcie_Phase(#PCIE_PH_ENUM)
  lo = PcieCfgRead32(1, 0, 0, #PCI_CFG_BAR0)
  is64 = 0
  If (lo & #PCI_BAR_TYPE_MASK) = #PCI_BAR_TYPE_64
    is64 = 1
  EndIf

  sz = PcieBarSize(1, 0, 0, #PCI_CFG_BAR0)
  If sz = 0
    pcie_err = #PCIE_ERR_BAR_SIZE
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If sz > #PCIE_OUT_SIZE
    pcie_err = #PCIE_ERR_BAR_SPACE
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  pcie_barSize = sz

  ; The bottom of the window. A BAR must be naturally aligned and the
  ; window base is 64 MiB-aligned already, so any size up to the window
  ; is aligned there for free - asserted rather than assumed, because
  ; the day the window moves this stops being true silently.
  pcie_barBus = #PCIE_OUT_BUS
  If (pcie_barBus & (sz - 1)) <> 0
    pcie_err = #PCIE_ERR_BAR_SPACE
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  PcieCfgWrite32(1, 0, 0, #PCI_CFG_BAR0, pcie_barBus & $FFFFFFF0)
  If is64 <> 0
    ; The high half. Zero here: the window's bus view is below 4 GB even
    ; though the CPU reaches it above. Two different address spaces, and
    ; this register belongs to the bus one.
    PcieCfgWrite32(1, 0, 0, #PCI_CFG_BAR1, 0)
  EndIf

  ; MEMORY SPACE FIRST, then bus master. Until the memory-space bit is
  ; set the device does not answer a read at its own BAR at all - which
  ; is exactly the abort that started this. Bus master is separate and
  ; is about the device reaching US; XHCI needs both, because its rings
  ; live in our DRAM.
  cmd = PcieCfgRead32(1, 0, 0, #PCI_CFG_COMMAND)
  cmd = cmd | #PCI_CMD_MEM_SPACE | #PCI_CMD_BUS_MASTER
  PcieCfgWrite32(1, 0, 0, #PCI_CFG_COMMAND, cmd)

  ; --- AND THE BRIDGE HAS TO AGREE TO CARRY IT ------------------------
  ; TWO MISCONFIGURATIONS STACKED HERE AND EACH HID THE NEXT. With the
  ; endpoint enumerated but this step missing, a read of the BAR came
  ; back $DEADDEAD - the root complex's own AXI read-error pattern, not
  ; a crash and not a bus float. The CPU access reached the RC; the RC
  ; declined to complete it.
  ;
  ; The root complex is a PCI-TO-PCI BRIDGE, and a bridge forwards a
  ; memory transaction downstream only if it lands inside the
  ; MEMORY_BASE/LIMIT pair in its own config header. Programming the
  ; outbound window told the CPU side where to translate. Nothing told
  ; the bridge to carry it. Both are required, they live in different
  ; address spaces, and having one without the other looks exactly like
  ; a missing page-table entry - which is what it was blamed on twice.
  ;
  ; MEASURED before the fix: base/limit read $C000C000, a leftover from
  ; the firmware pointing at $C0000000, while the BAR had just been put
  ; at $F8000000. The bridge was doing its job correctly.
  ;
  ; Format: two 16-bit fields at $20, each carrying address bits 31:20 in
  ; its top 12 bits. The limit is INCLUSIVE - it names the last megabyte
  ; in the range, not the first one past it.
  Define memwin.i
  Define lastB.i
  lastB = pcie_barBus + #PCIE_OUT_SIZE - 1
  memwin = ((pcie_barBus >> 16) & $FFF0) | ((((lastB) >> 16) & $FFF0) << 16)
  PcieCfgWrite32(0, 0, 0, $20, memwin)

  ; And the bridge itself must be enabled - the same two COMMAND bits,
  ; on the bridge this time rather than on the endpoint.
  Define bcmd.i
  bcmd = PcieCfgRead32(0, 0, 0, #PCI_CFG_COMMAND)
  PcieCfgWrite32(0, 0, 0, #PCI_CFG_COMMAND, bcmd | #PCI_CMD_MEM_SPACE | #PCI_CMD_BUS_MASTER)

  pcie_barCpu = PcieCpuFromBus(pcie_barBus)
  pcie_Phase(#PCIE_PH_DONE)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; Where the endpoint's registers ended up. 0 before PcieEnumerate.
Procedure.i PcieBarBus()
  ProcedureReturn pcie_barBus
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PcieBarCpu()
  ProcedureReturn pcie_barCpu
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PcieBarBytes()
  ProcedureReturn pcie_barSize
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PcieEndpointReady - is it safe to dereference the BAR yet?
;
;  Exists because the alternative to asking is a data abort and a
;  reboot, and that is a slow way to learn. Checks the two things that
;  were wrong when this library crashed the board: a BAR with an actual
;  address in it, and the memory-space bit set.
;
;  It CANNOT tell you the loader mapped the outbound window - nothing
;  reachable through config space can. That question is still open and
;  is answered only by trying.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieEndpointReady()
  Define cmd.i
  Define bar.i
  If pcie_up = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  bar = PcieCfgRead32(1, 0, 0, #PCI_CFG_BAR0)
  If bar < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (bar & $FFFFFFF0) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  cmd = PcieCfgRead32(1, 0, 0, #PCI_CFG_COMMAND)
  If cmd < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (cmd & #PCI_CMD_MEM_SPACE) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE INBOUND OFFSET - and why assuming it was zero cost a night
; ======================================================================
;  ADDED 2026-08-25, after XhciInit reached Run/Stop and came back with
;  USBSTS $15: HCHalted, PORT CHANGE, and HOST SYSTEM ERROR.
;
;  HSE is the controller reporting that its OWN bus access failed. The
;  first thing an xHC does after Run/Stop is fetch the Device Context
;  Base Address Array out of host memory - so the complaint was about
;  the INBOUND direction, the endpoint reading us, at a moment when
;  reading the endpoint had been working perfectly for hours.
;
;  THE ROOT COMPLEX'S INBOUND REGISTER HOLDS AN OFFSET, NOT A BASE, and
;  the difference is the whole bug. From
;  Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_pcie_brcmstb.c, brcm_pcie_probe():
;
;      rc_bar2_offset = region.bus_start - region.phys_start;
;      tmp = lower_32_bits(rc_bar2_offset);
;      u32p_replace_bits(&tmp, brcm_pcie_encode_ibar_size(rc_bar2_size),
;                        RC_BAR2_CONFIG_LO_SIZE_MASK);
;      writel(tmp, base + PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR2_CONFIG_LO);
;      writel(upper_32_bits(rc_bar2_offset),
;             base + PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR2_CONFIG_HI);
;
;  So the pair is: LOW 5 bits of LO are a SIZE CODE, the remaining bits
;  of LO plus all of HI are the OFFSET that gets ADDED to a system
;  address to produce the PCI bus address the device must be given.
;
;  ON THIS BOARD, READ BACK OFF THE SILICON:
;
;      RC_BAR2_CONFIG_LO  $00000011     size code 17, offset bits 0
;      RC_BAR2_CONFIG_HI  $00000004     offset bits 63:32 = 4
;
;  Offset $4_0000_0000. Size code 17 is log2 - 15, so 1 << 32 - a 4 GiB
;  window. A buffer at ARM physical $480120 is reachable by the device
;  only as bus address $4_0048_0120, and handing it the bare $480120
;  puts the read outside the window entirely.
;
;  WHY THIS FILE BELIEVED OTHERWISE. PcieInit's own bring-up path
;  programs RC_BAR2 with offset zero and a 1 GiB size, which IS an
;  identity map, and the comment beside it said so. That is true and
;  irrelevant: this board always takes the ADOPT path, because the
;  firmware brings the link up to boot from USB and PcieInit returns
;  early rather than resetting a bus that is already carrying the boot
;  device. On the adopt path the offset is WHOEVER SET IT UP - here
;  U-Boot - and it is not zero.
;
;  A comment describing one branch of a two-branch function, read as if
;  it described the function, is a fair summary of how this hid.
;
;  SO: READ IT, DO NOT ASSUME IT. PcieDmaBus() below is the conversion
;  every DMA address must go through, on either path. It is deliberately
;  a different procedure from PcieBusFromCpu(), which converts in the
;  OUTBOUND direction for BAR programming and has nothing to do with
;  this - confusing the two is exactly the sort of thing that produces a
;  plausible number and a dead controller.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

Global pcie_dmaOff.i           ; bus = phys + this. Read from the RC.
Global pcie_dmaOffRead.i       ; 1 once it has actually been read

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PcieReadDmaOffset - pull the inbound offset out of the root complex.
;  Called by PcieInit on BOTH paths. Safe to call again.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure PcieReadDmaOffset()
  Define lo.i
  Define hi.i
  lo = PcieRcPeek(#PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR2_CONFIG_LO) & $FFFFFFFF
  hi = PcieRcPeek(#PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR2_CONFIG_HI) & $FFFFFFFF
  ; The low five bits are the size code and are NOT part of the offset.
  pcie_dmaOff = ((hi << 32) | (lo & $FFFFFFE0))
  pcie_dmaOffRead = 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PcieDmaOffset - what gets added to a system address for the device.
;  Zero is a legitimate answer; it means the window is identity-mapped.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieDmaOffset()
  ; Lazy, because PcieInit sits ABOVE this in the file and the host
  ; will not call forward. Reading on demand also means the value is
  ; whatever the register says NOW, which matters on the adopt path
  ; where somebody else configured it.
  If pcie_dmaOffRead = 0
    PcieReadDmaOffset()
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn pcie_dmaOff
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PcieDmaWindowSize - how much memory the inbound window covers, from
;  the size code. 0 means the window is DISABLED and no DMA can work.
;
;  The encoding is brcm_pcie_encode_ibar_size() run backwards:
;      codes $1C..$1F  ->  4 KiB .. 32 KiB   (code - $1C + 12)
;      codes 1..22     ->  64 KiB .. 32 GiB  (code + 15)
;      code 0          ->  disabled
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieDmaWindowSize()
  Define code.i
  code = PcieRcPeek(#PCIE_MISC_RC_BAR2_CONFIG_LO) & $1F
  If code = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If code >= $1C
    ProcedureReturn 1 << ((code - $1C) + 12)
  EndIf
  If code <= 22
    ProcedureReturn 1 << (code + 15)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PcieDmaBus - THE conversion. Give it a system address, get the bus
;  address the device must be told about.
;
;  REFUSES with #PCIE_ERR_RANGE rather than returning a plausible
;  number, on the same principle as PcieCpuFromBus: a DMA address that
;  is quietly wrong produces a controller that reports a Host System
;  Error hours later, with nothing to connect it back to here.
;
;  Call PcieDmaOk() as well - they answer different questions. This one
;  asks "what address does the device use", that one asks "is the
;  device allowed to reach it at all", and the 3 GiB erratum means the
;  answer to the second can be no even when the first succeeds.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PcieDmaBus(physAddr.i)
  If pcie_dmaOffRead = 0
    PcieReadDmaOffset()
  EndIf
  If physAddr < 0
    ProcedureReturn #PCIE_ERR_RANGE
  EndIf
  ; A window size of zero means the inbound path is switched off. Any
  ; address at all is wrong in that state, so refuse rather than hand
  ; back an address that cannot possibly be serviced.
  If PcieDmaWindowSize() = 0
    ProcedureReturn #PCIE_ERR_RANGE
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn physAddr + pcie_dmaOff
EndProcedure
